#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0086e1d8-f0ee-53c6-8443-35f56170d9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256787} {:answer/id #uuid "0d67fff4-252d-5baa-b919-6e2e71d71094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256912} {:answer/id #uuid "80d9c10d-d8ce-5ef8-9f6d-73c22d791bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257926} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f2b7b2-0f28-5166-96d2-007456d5faee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a8a822-04f8-5fb6-8bb9-09b853fcf229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0f2b7b2-0f28-5166-96d2-007456d5faee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} :answer-field/id #uuid "79824dff-905f-590c-af91-7c9745e11bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24203ad0-c666-584c-ace2-35e7e7f927ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124117" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+1+(-8)=-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bed1340-8927-5ff0-ba69-b2b93cdd78e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256896} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb632e-14da-5038-b6f9-74ec0da963a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257937} {:answer/id #uuid "82d7f633-617d-5497-a90c-a0550510c2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257941} {:answer/id #uuid "d5507f11-f2dc-54ed-aa00-8c211067e5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} {:answer/id #uuid "e3817db8-3192-5a2c-ad51-eb7cb8e7b55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5507f11-f2dc-54ed-aa00-8c211067e5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258649} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa29d767-9911-5a05-a828-3b3c81faca3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124280" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23580040-99be-5f75-be43-adefd3cbfade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} {:answer/id #uuid "58e9a951-d5aa-5d93-8ae3-c1349449a5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257554} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc83444-b44c-54bf-83b7-307d0775c0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258020} {:answer/id #uuid "b1de513c-2a81-54b9-9e95-c28eb012ca2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258331} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c13e37-05bf-5042-b338-1fc4c401c1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23580040-99be-5f75-be43-adefd3cbfade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3cd215d-51f0-56ca-9929-c005182aeb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d452c80-d748-5af8-bbed-4f41e3a6ab8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124314" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v+2,\\,y=6v-3u-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=x+by+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v+2 \\\\ 6v-3u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v \\\\ -3u+6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x-2)+(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (y+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+y \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+0,\\,v=x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411cf09f-aaa8-5213-a15d-e483b24650c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A tetrahedron" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279170} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc0aa9-2d9d-5344-8ce5-d4d954e92fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279807} {:answer/id #uuid "99cffdbb-a38c-5a63-9d04-87167a638bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279951} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f74625-2c51-5fb6-83b9-61fbae0292bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280479} {:answer/id #uuid "e51121c0-fae9-532e-b1aa-79a388ca1f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87fc0aa9-2d9d-5344-8ce5-d4d954e92fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279807} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec2e96d-de39-5f66-aab1-77904959b9e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a795336-4ab0-5101-bbca-aa94f4ca2a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-162214" :question/problem "![](images/00/00151dc7a58722a649a52494434950695a27cb4e7cad14f32f76abbddf629a91.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u-3v+2,\\,y=4v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-3v+2 \\\\ 4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-3v \\\\ 4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc064fc-557b-51b4-acc4-5e5aac84e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406956} {:answer/id #uuid "394dd73f-cf48-54c7-81fb-4dd53a787045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} {:answer/id #uuid "72653cf4-8cbf-523e-b1d8-e878cfd112ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406961} {:answer/id #uuid "c61e03b3-7008-5b09-9b8a-dd9dc056f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406964} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9f565-9eda-5c15-bc7f-e57e8c6bdef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394dd73f-cf48-54c7-81fb-4dd53a787045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406957} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eef93f6-f290-530b-8af5-dd127f8894d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "353e50fd-8521-51a5-ae98-273dc887e6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162221" :question/problem "![](images/99/99b3d68b7f0c89ba430b098f9e7fda95f3013f310060dd71cf86eacc6e51afa7.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."}]}
